[{:db/id -1
  :adr/id "adr-2609111155-the-wired-lan-is-fully-accounted-for-and-the-6600hs-is-not-on-it"
  :adr/title "有線 LAN の 14 host は全部素性が付き、6600HS はそこに居ない"
  :adr/status "accepted"
  :adr/date "2026-09-11"
  :adr/body "## Context\n\nAn AMD Ryzen 5 6600HS box was installed from the aiueos node stick (aiueos\nADR-0214). The install completed and the owner logged in at its console, but\ntailscale was not installed, and the request was to reach the box through `gad`\nand set it up.\n\nIt could not be reached, and answering \"where is it\" turned into an inventory\nnobody had: the wired LAN had never been written down, so every session that\nneeded to find a machine on it started from zero.\n\n## What was measured, 2026-09-11\n\nSwept 192.168.1.0/24 for TCP/22 from TWO vantage points -- `gad` (192.168.1.16,\nwired) and this workstation (192.168.1.3, same wire). Both return the same 14\nhosts. Every one is now named:\n\n    192.168.1.1                      router\n    192.168.1.4    ubuntu-server     ASRock B850M Pro RS WiFi White / Ryzen 5 7500F\n    192.168.1.6    aiueos-node       GMKtec K16 / Ryzen 7 7735HS, TWO r8169 NICs\n    192.168.1.16   gad               EVO-X2\n    192.168.1.17-.26                 Mac mini x10 (OUI 1c:f6:4c)\n    192.168.1.28   xavier            NVIDIA, Ubuntu 20.04\n\n**The 6600HS is not among them**, before or after a reboot: the sweep returns\nthe identical set, and a ping sweep plus neighbour-table dump on `gad` produces\nno MAC that was not already there. It is also not on the tailnet, which follows\nfrom tailscale not being installed on it.\n\n## How an unknown host is identified here\n\nWritten down because it was reconstructed from scratch, and because two of the\nthree steps are cheap and neither is guessing.\n\n1. **SSH host key against `~/.ssh/known_hosts`.** A host key is a machine\n   identity. `192.168.1.28` carried the same ed25519 key as two other entries --\n   `100.87.226.80` and `xavier.tail110d8b.ts.net` -- which names it outright.\n2. **MAC OUI against nmap's database**\n   (`/opt/homebrew/share/nmap/nmap-mac-prefixes`): `48:B0:2D` is Nvidia. Local,\n   offline, no lookup service.\n3. **SSH banner for the distribution.** `OpenSSH_8.2p1 Ubuntu-4ubuntu0.7` is\n   Ubuntu 20.04; `9.6p1 Ubuntu-3ubuntu13.19` is 24.04.\n\n`ssh -J gad <user>@<lan-ip>` works and is how the wired hosts were asked their\nown hostname -- tailscale SSH relays `-W`, so the jump host needs no key of\nours and the local key does the authenticating.\n\n## What is durable here and what is not\n\n**The IP addresses are a snapshot and will rot.** What identifies these machines\nacross reboots and DHCP is the host key and the MAC. The one identity worth\nquoting later:\n\n    xavier   ssh-ed25519 AAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAIODBvVAzp+R57uMKNST7uPUatWbUTNk7elLXTgly1dF5\n             MAC 48:b0:2d:18:4d:16 (OUI Nvidia)\n             tailnet 100.87.226.80, ports 22 and 111 only\n\nDo not re-derive the table above by reading this file. Re-run the sweep.\n\n## What this does NOT claim\n\n- **xavier's model is not measured.** The name and the Nvidia OUI and Ubuntu\n  20.04 are consistent with a Jetson AGX Xavier, and that is an inference.\n  `root`, `nvidia`, `ubuntu` and `xavier` were all refused (publickey and\n  password both), so `/proc/device-tree/model` -- which would settle it -- is\n  unread.\n- **The 6600HS's absence is not a diagnosis.** \"Not on the wire\" is consistent\n  with no cable, a NIC Ubuntu cannot drive, no DHCP lease, or the box sitting on\n  a WiFi segment this router keeps apart from the wire. Which one is a question\n  for the hardware record the install wrote on the box itself\n  (`/var/log/aiueos-node-hwprobe.txt`), not for another sweep.\n- **No claim about WiFi.** This workstation and `gad` are both on the wire, so a\n  separate WiFi subnet would be invisible to both. The absence measured here is\n  an absence from 192.168.1.0/24 and nothing wider.\n\n## Noted while doing this, not fixed\n\n`scripts/adr-new.cljs` and `scripts/adr-inventory.cljs` both scan\n`90-docs/adr/*.edn`. On disk today there are **3 `.edn` and 2,926 `.kotoba`**,\nand both extensions received new ADRs on 2026-09-11 -- so the id allocator's\ncollision check does not see 2,926 of the ids it is guarding against, and the\ninventory loop reads three files. This ADR kept its `.edn` because that is what\nthe documented entry point produces and it is in current use. Recorded rather\nthan fixed: it is a separate change with its own evidence."}]
